## Chapter 1.1: Crime in the Future

It took about 15 minutes for Luka to journey on foot to the ROVD station. He could have waited for the routine buses but he simply liked walking. Still, even how he would love to enjoy the city atmosphere the -25 C of Siberian winter said otherwise. Every gush of wind that rushed to his face slowly turning him into a walking snowman.

The ROVD was housed in a sturdy, four-story neoclassical stone building constructed during the mid-twentieth century but now modernized with aerogel-insulated windows, doors, and walls. Luka stared at the Soviet Union emblem on top of the door, trying to ask himself one last time whether he wanted to this. After a brief, last-minute confirmation, he pushed the door open.

The warm air from the inside rushed out to meet him, he couldn’t help but to standing there, relax, and let the body adjust to the new temperature. The radiators here were definitely higher grade than the one in his dorm. The foyer smelled of floor wax, slight hint of tobacco, and a familiar herbal tea. 

After taking in the atmosphere, Luka observed the surrounding. The station seemed rather empty. Of course, this is the first time he ever visited a Soviet station, he only moved here a year prior for research, the rest of his life before was in another motherland, China. Still, considering the Time Ripple catastrophe, he expected a busy station with officers wildly running around. At least that what was his experience during the first month post-Ripple. It’s been 3 months in, but he doubt the Militsiya will ran out of mission.

There were only two officers present in the station, one was sitting there drinking his morning coffee and scrolling through his mobile phone, and the other was a desk officer already looking right a him, adjusting herself and preparing for their interaction.

Since their eyes already met, Luka walked straight to the front desk.

Once he walked close enough, the desk officer invited him to sit. Up close, Luka noticed more details about her. She looked young, probably around the same age. Her chestnut hair tied neatly into an updo. Her crisp dark blue uniform with a red collar piping, unmistakably a Millitsiya uniform. Her shoulder insignia showed a single red vertical stripe bearing one small golden star. Overall, she looked as prim and proper as one expected from an officer.

Luka sat down. When both of their eyes are on the same level. She asked,
“Good morning, comrade. Is there anything we can help you with?”
Luka eyes darted to the side, should he get straight to the point or started with some context first? Though, what’s the point of beating around the bush?
“…I’m here to report a crime… I mean- ah- how should I say this…” It didn’t feel right saying it out loud. Reporting a crime that didn’t happen (yet) or probably won’t happen was deeply embarrassing.
“It’s okay, take it slow. I understand you must feel overwhelmed but I’m here to help. Can you start by telling me what type of crime? Is there anyone in immediate danger?” The officer, trained in deescalation and emotional comfort, guided him through process.
Luka gradually felt more at ease, his shoulder relaxed. He took a deep breath and said,
“It’s an assault, or worst, a murder. I don’t really know exactly it hap-” Luka was suddenly cut off with a bewilderment look on the officer face.
“Assault!? Murder!? I’m sorry to interrupt but when and where have you witnessed this? We need to send a dispatch right away!”
Luka realized that he led himself into a dead end. There was no assault, there was no murder. Everything was in his head. But if he didn’t make this clear, he would only be wasting everyone’s time.
“I-I… um…” even how ridiculous it may sound, he finally admitted, “Would you believe me if I say that I saw it in a vision?”
The officer’s face gradually changed into a suspicion and confusion,
“…A vision? Do you mean, it hadn’t happen in real life?”
If his theory is correct, it will definitely happen in real life. Still, it was just a theory and right now he just have to explain himself and hope that he won’t be chased away.
“I know… I must sound like a mad man right now, but it’s been three months since I was prescribed various type of anti-hallucinogen and neuro-stabilizer. None of it helped.”
She pondered for a bit before saying,
“I’m not a healthcare professional but have you take the medicine as recommended? And even so, maybe you ought to give it more time.”
“Trust me, comrade officer. I’ve tried that but no matter what I do. The vision. It’s always coming back when I looked at him…” Luka tried to argue for his perceived sanity, or what ever remained in her mind now.
She glanced up and down the weary Luka, trying to asses his body language. It was clear to her that he was completely serious. Besides, according to his name plate, a theoretical physicist would be the last to believe in a premonition. He must truly be desperate.
“So, you looked at someone and you see their future?” She asked.
“Y-Yes. He is a fellow researcher, and my best friend,” he nervously replied.
Her curiosity got better of her and she slipped, “Well… Can you see _my_ future, in that case?” As she realized that it was unprofessional she backtracked, “-Ah, sorry. That was inappropriate-”
Luka cut her off, realizing that this might be the chance that he could prove to her and himself the premonition theory. He said, “Actually, I could try. I might space out for a bit, though.”
She adjusted herself, “Oh. Alright. Please proceed,” she was a bit surprised that he agreed to it.

Luka focused intently at her. His vision slowly melt away…
